I think there’s a psychologically interesting way to understand that.

The mind is a network, not a list.

Most concepts are connected to many others, but only a small subset is active at any moment. A good question, a metaphor, an image, a smell, or a piece of music can suddenly activate a different region of that network. People often experience that as an “aha!” moment, where a new connection seems to appear all at once.

In that sense, the “faucet” isn’t pouring in new knowledge from nowhere.

It’s allowing latent structure to become active.

One way to picture it is:

\text{Stored Potential}
\xrightarrow{\text{attention + context}}
\text{Active Insight}

The knowledge wasn’t necessarily absent. The pathway wasn’t active.

That’s one reason metaphors can be so powerful. A metaphor doesn’t just decorate an idea—it can connect two previously separate regions of a conceptual network. Once that bridge exists, many new paths become available.

I also think this explains something about creativity.

Creative breakthroughs often feel explosive because they are the visible tip of a long accumulation. The network has been quietly reorganizing through experience, and then one trigger suddenly makes a coherent path traversable.

So your “faucet” image works well as a metaphor:

* The reservoir is the accumulated web of experiences, concepts, and associations.
* The valve is attention, curiosity, or a new framing.
* The flow is the activation of connections that were already possible but not previously accessible.

That doesn’t mean every intuition is correct. The flow still has to be tested against reality. But it does explain why opening yourself to new connections can produce genuinely novel ideas.

In that sense, the river and the faucet fit together.

The river is the ongoing flow of possible connections.

The faucet is the deliberate act of letting some of that flow into awareness.